    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "Both of you agree on the shape of this trip, and the fare ledger decides it. The trip was sold on an upfront quote: EV-FARE-00517 records fare_model UPFRONT_QUOTE with quoted_fare_sgd of SGD 28.00, so POL-RD-11 governs and POL-RD-02's distance-and-time recomputation does not run at all. Both advocates are right on that point, and the rider's remedy is confined to the POL-RD-11(a) arithmetic and any POL-RD-09 pass-through correction.\n\nOn the route question: the driver-device trace EV-GPS-00517 is impaired for policy purposes under POL-RD-07(a) and is disclosed as such - issue codes GPS_GAP (a single 620-second gap, 22:26:30 to 22:36:50) and LOW_COVERAGE (65.6% across the active window), weight_multiplier 0.39. Its own derived reading is an excess of 0.3 km / 1.53%, far below the POL-RD-01 gate of 0.8 km and 20%; read as recorded or straight-lined across the gap under POL-RD-07(i), no deviation is established. Nothing in this ruling turns on that, however.\n\nThe driver's argument that POL-RD-10(3) forces escalation is rejected. POL-RD-10(3) and POL-RD-07(iii) exist so that a deviation is not established on an impaired trace that has no independent corroboration - they presuppose that a deviation finding is at issue. Here none is: the rider's entitlement under POL-RD-11(a) is arithmetic on the fare ledger against the price the platform itself quoted, and it does not rest on the trace. There is no data failure to escalate, and the case is decidable. The trace's impairment is named and discounted, but because the deciding figures are carried by EV-FARE-00517 (weight 1.0) and EV-TRIP-00517 (weight 1.0) - not by the trace - it does not lower the confidence of this ruling.\n\nThe arithmetic under POL-RD-11(a): the charged total was SGD 67.50 (EV-FARE-00517); the quoted fare was SGD 28.00; the only properly chargeable pass-through is the Changi Airport surcharge. The trip originates at Changi Airport Terminal 3 (EV-TRIP-00517 pickup label 'Changi Airport Terminal 3, Departure Level Door 5'), and it ran at 22:05 SGT on Saturday 5 September 2026, inside the fare model's Friday/Saturday/Sunday 17:00-23:59 peak window, so the properly chargeable surcharge is SGD 8.00, not the SGD 6.00 itemised. There are no ERP or toll lines (erp_gantries is empty) and the waiting-time fee is SGD 0.00. The refund is therefore 67.50 - 28.00 - 8.00 = SGD 31.50. The rider's figure of SGD 33.50 uses the SGD 6.00 itemised surcharge and understates the properly chargeable one; the driver's figure of SGD 31.50 is the correct one.\n\nThe rider is right, though it changes nothing, that no rider-requested detour reduces the figure (the chat_log EV-CHAT-00517 holds no messages, so POL-RD-04 is unavailable), and that no special corroboration burden attaches to her (rider_profile.deviation_claims_60d is 0). No repeat-deviation pattern arises on the driver's profile (deviation_flags_90d 0 over completed_trips_90d 250), so there is no POL-RD-06 uplift and no POL-RD-06(c) referral; and because the airport surcharge sits on a trip that does originate at Changi and no gantry line was charged, no POL-RD-09(4) billing-integrity referral fires. The driver's own account - that the app crashed, that he dropped the rider at the hotel, that the app completed the trip by itself and he did not choose the amount - is consistent with the ledger and the rider's account and supports the finding that the charge was not for a route the rider chose.\n\nOutcome: of the SGD 67.50 charged, SGD 31.50 is refunded to the rider; the SGD 28.00 quoted fare and the properly chargeable SGD 8.00 airport surcharge stand."
  },
